e that worked best for our experiments; then the camera is kept in the same location for all experiments, across all tasks. In this section, we conduct a quantitative analysis of the V-JEPA 2-AC world model’s sensitivity to camera position. While ideally, the model’s inferred coordinate axis would be invariant to camera position, here we observe that the model’s inferred coordinate axis is sensitive to the camera position; this is problematic as large errors in the inferred coordinate axis can degrade success rate on downstream tasks.\nWe sweep several camera positions around the robot base, which we describe as a clockwise angular position around the center of the table, with 0 degrees being located at the robot base, and 90 degrees being left of the robot base. Since we train on the left exocentric camera views from the Droid dataset, we sweep camera positions between roughly 35 degrees and 85 degrees. Next, for each camera position, we collect a 201 step trajectory of random robot movements within the horizontal x-y plane. For each pair of adjacent frames in this 201 step trajectory, we compute the optimal action inferred by V-JEPA 2-AC, i.e., the action that minimizes the energy function in eq. (5) given a 1-step rollout. This allows us to construct a dataset for\n40\n34 36 38 40 42 44 46 48 50 52 54 56 58 60 62 64 66 68 70 72 74 76 78 80\n10\n20\n30\n相机位置(度)\nRotation Error (degrees)\n推断坐标轴的旋转误差与相机位置的关系\n图 16 对相机位置的敏感性。V‐JEPA 2‐AC 推断的动作坐标轴的旋转误差(在 x‐y 平面内)随相机位置的变化关系,其 中 0 度对应相机位于机器人基座处,90 度对应相机位于机器人基座左侧。虽然理想情况下模型推断的坐标轴应对相机位 置保持不变,但在此我们观察到模型推断的坐标轴对相机位置较为敏感。\n解码表示 在帧级别(而非视频级别)进行,是为了更好地利用解码器作为 可解释性工具 用于分析 V‐JEPA 2‐AC 在一组机器人动作序列上的推演结果。 在图15a中,我们展示了实验室中机器人真实轨迹的视频帧(顶行)、每一帧经解码的V‐JEPA 2编码器表征 (中间行),以及使用真实动作序列和单个起始帧作为上下文、经解码的V‐JEPA 2‐AC世界模型推演结果 (底行)。V‐JEPA 2表征的重建(中间行)表明,编码器捕捉到了基于视觉的控制所必需场景中的显著部 分;背景生成模糊的部分原因可归咎于我们前馈帧解码器的低容量。V‐JEPA 2‐AC推演的重建表明,动作条 件世界模型成功地让机器人动了起来,同时保持背景和未交互物体(如架子)不受影响。我们还看到,在夹 爪闭合的情况下,模型正确地预测了杯子随机械臂的运动,这表明模型对直觉物理(如物体恒常性、形状恒 常性和重力)有合理的理解,但我们也确实观察到误差累积,世界模型预测的杯子位置在最后一帧中略低于 真实轨迹中的位置。在图15b中,我们探究了当用相同的动作序列驱动模型时V‐JEPA 2‐AC预测的变化,但 一种情况使用闭合夹爪(顶行),另一种使用张开夹爪(底行)。当使用张开夹爪的动作序列时,世界模型 预测杯子的位置在各时间步上保持不变。\nB.4 评估对相机位置的敏感性\n在实践中,我们在确定最适合实验的相机位置之前手动尝试了不同位置;随后在所有任务的所有实验中,相 机均保持在同一位置。在本节中,我们对 V‐JEPA 2‐AC 世界模型对相机位置的敏感性进行了定量分析。理 想情况下,模型推断的坐标轴应对相机位置保持不变,但在此我们观察到模型推断的坐标轴对相机位置较为 敏感;这一问题值得关注,因为推断坐标轴中的较大误差会降低下游任务的成功率。\n我们围绕机器人底座扫描了若干相机位置,将其描述为围绕桌子中心的顺时针角位置,其中0度位于机器人 底座处,90度位于机器人底座左侧。由于我们使用Droid数据集中的左侧外中心相机视角进行训练,因此我 们在约35度到85度之间扫描相机位置。接下来,对于每个相机位置,我们收集一条在水平x‐y平面内随机机 器人运动的201步轨迹。对于这条201步轨迹中的每对相邻帧,我们计算由V‐JEPA 2‐AC推断出的最优动作, 即在给定1步展开的情况下使公式(5)中能量函数最小化的动作。这使我们能够构建一个数据集,用于\n40\n\n\n each camera position consisting of real action versus inferred action pairs. We only focus on the ∆x and ∆y cartesian control actions (first two dimensions of the action vector) for our analysis. Let A ∈ R200×2 denote the inferred actions and B ∈ R200×2 denote the ground truth actions. Based on this, we can solve a linear least squares problem to identify the linear transformation W ⋆ ∈ R2×2 that maps inferred actions A to real actions B, W ⋆ = argmin\nW ∈R2×2\n∥AW − B∥2.\nThe mean absolute prediction error for all camera position is roughly 1.6cm (compared to a ground truth delta pose of roughly 5cm), suggesting that the error is systematic. In addition, we observe that for each camera position, the matrix W ⋆ has condition number ≈ 1.5, i.e., modulo a fixed scalar coefficient, W ⋆ is approximately a rotation matrix, and thus we can compute the rotation error in the inferred coordinate axis by using\nW ⋆ ≈ W ⋆ = cos θ − sin θ\nsin θ cos θ ,\nwith W ⋆ := U V ⊤ where U and V are the left and right singular vectors of W ⋆, respectively.\nFigure 16shows the camera position plotted against the rotation error in the V-JEPA 2-AC inferred coordinate axis. We observe that the rotation error in the inferred coordinate axis is almost a linear function of the camera position. We can most clearly see the effects of rotation errors in the inferred coordinate axis in our single-goal reaching experiments inFigure 8. While the model is always able to move the arm within 4 cm of the goal based on visual feedback from the monocular RGB camera, rotation errors in the inferred coordinate axis result in relatively suboptimal actions at each planning step, yielding a non-maximal, albeit monotonic, decrease in the distance to goal at each step.\nInterestingly, since errors in the inferred coordinate axis are primarily rotation-based, one can use this approach to “calibrate” their world model by simply rotating all inferred actions by W ⋆, and thereby introduce the desired invariance to camera position. Such an unsupervised calibration phase would involve the robot performing random actions, solving a linear least squares problem by comparing its inferred optimal actions to the actual actions it executed, and then multiplying its inferred actions by the rotation matrix before sending them to the controller during task execution. While such an approach is interesting, we emphasize that we do no such calibration in our experiments.\nCVisual Classification\nWe describe in more detail the evaluation procedure used for the classification tasks described inSection 5.\nC.1Hyperparameters\nProbe Architecture. We train an attentive probe on top of the frozen encoder output using the training data from each downstream task. Our attentive probe is composed of four transformer blocks, each using 16 heads in the attention layer. The first three blocks use standard self-attention; the final block uses a cross-attention layer with a learnable query token. The output of the cross-attention layer in the final block is added back to the query token as a residual connection before applying the rest of the block (LayerNorm, followed by MLP with a single GeLU activation). The transformer blocks are followed by a final linear classifier layer.\nEvaluation setup parameters. All models follow the same evaluation protocol and use a resolution of 256 × 256, except our V-JEPA 2 ViT-g384. For video evaluations, we sampled multiple clip segments from each input video. During validation, we also extracted three spatial views from each segment (instead of one view during training). The number of clip segments, frame step parameter, and global batch size vary for each eval; parameters used for each evaluation can be found inTable 15. By default, we use 16 × 2 × 3 inputs for SSv2 (16 frames clip, 2 temporal crops, 3 spatial crops), 16 × 8 × 3 for K400, 32 × 8 × 3 for COIN, and 32 × 4 × 3 for Diving-48 and Jester. V-JEPA 2 ViT-g384 uses a higher resolution of 384 × 384 for K400, COIN, Diving-48, and Jester, 512 × 512 for ImageNet and 384 × 384 with 64 × 2 × 3 inputs for SSv2.\n41\n每个相机位置由真实动作与推断动作对组成。在我们的分析中,我们仅关注 ∆x 和 ∆y笛卡尔控制动作(动 作向量的前两个维度)。令 A ∈ R200×2 表示推断动作, B ∈ R200×2 表示真实动作。在此基础上,我们可 以求解一个线性最小二乘问题,以确定将推断动作 A 映射到真实动作 B的线性变换 W ⋆ ∈ R2×2 ,\nW ⋆ = argmin\nW ∈R2×2\n∥AW − B∥2.\n所有相机位置的平均绝对预测误差约为1.6cm(相比约5cm的真值增量位姿),表明该误差是系统性的。此 外,我们观察到对于每个相机位置,矩阵 W ⋆ 的条件数为 ≈ 1.5,即模一个固定标量系数, W ⋆ 近似为一个 旋转矩阵,因此我们可以通过下式计算推断坐标轴中的旋转误差:\nW ⋆ ≈ W ⋆ = cos θ − sin θ\nsin θ cos θ ,\nwith W ⋆ := U V ⊤ 其中 U 和 V 分别是 W ⋆的左奇异向量和右奇异向量。\n图16展示了相机位置与V‐JEPA 2‐AC推断坐标轴中旋转误差的关系图。我们观察到推断坐标轴中的旋转误 差几乎是相机位置的线性函数。在单目标到达实验中(图8),我们可以最清楚地看到推断坐标轴中旋转误 差的影响。虽然模型始终能够基于单目RGB相机的视觉反馈将手臂移动到距目标4cm以内,但推断坐标轴中 的旋转误差导致每个规划步骤的动作相对次优,使得到目标的距离呈现非最大的、尽管是单调的减少。\n有趣的是,由于推断坐标轴中的误差主要源于旋转,人们可以利用这一方法通过将所有推断动作旋转 W ⋆ 来“校准”其世界模型,从而引入对相机位置所期望的不变性。这种无监督校准阶段将涉及机器人执行随机动 作,通过将其推断的最优动作与实际执行的动作进行比较来求解线性最小二乘问题,然后在任务执行期间将 推断动作乘以旋转矩阵后再发送给控制器。虽然这种方法很有趣,但我们强调在实验中并未进行此类校准。\nC 视觉分类\n我们更详细地描述第5节中所述分类任务所使用的评估流程。\nC.1 超参数\n探针架构。我们在冻结的编码器输出之上,使用每个下游任务的训练数据训练一个注意力探针。我们的注意 力探针由四个Transformer块组成,每个块在注意力层中使用16个注意力头。前三个块使用标准自注意力; 最后一个块使用带有可学习查询令牌的交叉注意力层。在应用该块的其余部分(LayerNorm,随后是带单个 GeLU激活的MLP)之前,最后一个块中交叉注意力层的输出通过残差连接加回查询令牌。Transformer块 之后是一个最终的线性分类器层。\n评估设置参数。所有模型遵循相同的评估协议并使用 256 × 256 的分辨率,除了我们的 V‐JEPA 2 ViT‐g384。对于 视频评估,我们从每个输入视频中采样了多个片段。在验证期间,我们还从每个片段中提取了三个空间视图(而 非训练期间的一个视图)。片段数量、帧步长参数和全局批次大小因每次评估而异;每次评估使用的参数可在表 15 中找到。默认情况下,我们对 SSv2 使用 16 × 2 × 3 输入(16 帧片段,2 个时间裁剪,3 个空间裁剪),对 K400 使用 16 × 8 × 3 ,对 COIN 使用 32 × 8 × 3 ,对 Diving‐48 和 Jester 使用 32 × 4 × 3 。V‐JEPA 2 ViT‐g384 对 K400、COIN、Diving‐48 和 Jester 使用更高的 384 × 384 分辨率,对 ImageNet 使用 512 × 512 , 对 SSv2 使用 384 × 384 及 64 × 2 × 3 输入。\n41\n\n\n Table 15 Visual Classification eval params. Default parameters used for the visual classification evaluations, with non-default values for each eval (* denotes default). All attentive probes use 4 transformer blocks with 16 heads.\nParameter Default (K400) ImageNet SSv2 COIN Jester/Diving-48\nNumber of frames 16 16 16 32 32 Segments / Clip 8 1 2 8 4 Views / Segment 3 1 * * * Frame Step 4 n/a * * 2 Epochs 20 * * * 100 Batch Size (global) 256 1024 * 128 128 Resolution 256 × 256 * * * * Classifier Heads 20 (4x5) * * * 3 (3x1) Classifier Learning Rates [5e-3 3e-3 1e-3 3e-4 1e-4] * * * [1e-3 3e-4 1e-4] Classifier Weight Decay [.8 .4 .1 .01] * * * [.8]\nTable 16 Input layers for Jester/Diving-48. For each encoder size, indices of the four encoder layers whose tokens are used as input to the linear classifier in the Jester and Diving-48 evaluations.\nEncoder # Layers Attended Layers\nViT-L 24 17, 19, 21, 23 ViT-H 32 25, 27, 29, 31 ViT-g 40 24, 29, 34, 39\nImageNet evaluation. For ImageNet, we repeat each input image to produce a 16-frame video clip. We also use a larger global batch size (1024 instead of 256 or 128), and do not use multiple clips or views per sample.\nJester and Diving-48 evaluation. Our Jester and Diving-48 action classification evaluation tasks differ from the other understanding evaluations in several ways, primarily in that we employ a multilayer strategy. Instead of attending to the tokens from only the last layer of the encoder, we extract tokens from four encoder layers (the last layer and three intermediate layers) and attend to all of them. (Table 16shows the layers we used for each encoder size.) We also train the probes for these two evaluations with only three classification heads (instead of 20